#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Our Idler’s Gossip|journal=w:[[Bell's Life in Sydney and Sporting Reviewer|Bell’s Life in Sydney and Sporting Chronicle]]|volume=XXVII|issue=708, new series|location=[Sydney, N.S.W.]|date=16 July 1870|page=&#91;3&#93;|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/928851309/|column=2|passage=“Will any gentleman '''please''' to get outside and make room for a lady?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=Part 1 [[publish]]ed in 1605, Part 2 in 1615, [[translate]]d in 2003|author=[[w:Miguel de Cervantes|Miguel de Cervantes]] and [[translate]]d by [[w:Edith Grossman|Edith Grossman]]|title=[[w:Don Quixote|Don Quixote]]|page=191-192|passage="I don't say it and I don't think it," responded Sancho. "It's their affair and let them eat it with their bread; whether or not they were lovers, they've already made their accounting with God; I tend to my vines, it's their business, not mine; I don't stick my nose in; if you buy and lie, your purse wants to know why. Besides, naked I was born, and naked I'll die: I don't lose or gain a thing; whatever they were, it's all the same to me. And many folks think there's bacon when there's not even a hook to hang it on. But who can put doors on a field? Let them say what they '''please''', I don't care.}}

