===Etymology 2===
Short for {{m|en|if you please}}, an intransitive, [[ergative]] form taken from {{m|en||if it please you}}<ref name="dictionary" /><ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> which is a {{calque|en|fr|s'il vous plaît|nocap=1}}, which replaced ''[[pray]]''. ''If it please you'' is a [[present subjunctive]] form, but most current uses of ''please'' are not parsed that way.

====Alternative forms====
* {{q|for the exaggerated way it is often pronounced as the expression of annoyance}} {{alt|en|puh-lease}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{senseid|en|preface to a polite request}} {{non-gloss|Used to make a polite request.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Please''', pass the bread.}}
#: {{ux|en|Would you '''please''' sign this form?}}
#: {{ux|en|Could you tell me the time, '''please'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|May I take your order, '''please'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|Q: Should I call him to confirm? A: '''Please''' do.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=20 September 1857|title=List of Letters|journal=Sunday Times|volume=I|issue=140|location=New Orleans, La.|publisher=Times Printing Association|page=566|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/1319978640/|column=2|oclc=19360120|passage=LIST OF LETTERS remaining in the Post-Office at New Orleans, September 19, 1857. Persons calling will '''please''' mention that they are advertised.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=Jul 10 1983|author=Berkeley Breathed|role=Yuri Andropov|title=Bloom County|passage=''(Michael)'': Yuri Andropov! What are you doing in my closet of anxieties again?<br />''(Yuri)'': Uh, oh.  This is not 1600 Pennsylvania Ave., Washington, D.C.?<br />''(Michael)'': Does it look like it? You're in the wrong nightmare again!!<br />''(Yuri)'': ★@#*!?! Soviet maps ... worth nothing!  Give, '''please''', directions to White House.|footer=Using the word in this position, padded by commas, is often the trait of a non-native speaker.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The word is most commonly found at the beginning of the clause, at its end, or directly before the verb. Any other positioning, especially when set off by a pause in speech, is often the sign of a non-native speaker.

