====Interjection====
{{head|en|interjection}}

# {{senseid|en|please repeat, say again}} {{lb|en|Cincinnati}} {{non-gloss|Said as a request to repeat information.}}<ref name="dareyou">[http://www.daredictionary.com/view/dare/ID_00044218 Dictionary of American Regional English]</ref>
#: {{syn|en|<!--similarly polite-->pardon#Interjection|pardon me|beg pardon|excuse me|;|<!--more direct-->say again|come again|what's that|what|;|Thesaurus:say again}}
#: {{ux|en|Customer while ordering: Can I get a [unintelligible]? Restaurant employee: '''Please'''?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Bitte or Bitter?|journal={{w|Cincinnati (magazine)|Cincinnati}}|year=August 1973|page=109|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CesCAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA109|passage=''Fellow:'' May I have a few days off to get married?<br>Reply, in the Cincinnati idiom by a boss who had heard the sound but not the sense:<br>''Boss'': '''Please'''?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Virginia Watson-Rouslin|title=A Foreign View|journal=Cincinnati|year=September 1978|page=110|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cesCAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA110|passage=Even though I heard it was supposed to be German-Catholic background, there’s only one thing German — they say ‘'''please'''’ [for the more common ‘pardon me’], which comes from ''bitte''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Winners: Contest No. 13—The Laugh’s On Us|journal=Cincinnati|year=September 1979|volume=12|issue=12|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=dusCAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA15|passage={{...}} He explained in broken English that one of his daughters was ill and he probably could not be there. I did not understand all that he said, so asked, ‘'''Please'''?’ per Cincinnati custom. ‘There is no need to plead. I will be there if she is feeling better,’ he replied.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jose I. Sarasua|title=Come to Cincinnati... Please?|journal=Cost Engineering|volume=40|issue=5|year=5 May 1998|page=9|url=http://connection.ebscohost.com/c/editorials/664754/come-cincinnati-please|passage=Cincinnati are some of the most polite persons I have ever met in the US. When asking someone a question, instead of saying “Excuse me,” or “Pardon,” they say “'''Please'''?”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jeff Robinson|title=Say what?|journal=Ohio Magazine|year=April 2001|page=77|url=http://lrc.ohio.edu/lrcmedia/Streaming/lingCALL/ling270/saywhat.pdf?page=2|text=By the same token, one contestant who doesn’t hear a particular question could say “Pardon me?” while another could say “'''Please'''?” Again, neither would be lying if he said he was from Ohio.|accessdate=19 December 2014|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190402204116/http://lrc.ohio.edu/lrcmedia/Streaming/lingCALL/ling270/saywhat.pdf?page=2|archivedate=2 April 2019}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=w:Henry Hitchings|title=The Secret Life of Words: How English Became English|isbn=0374254109|page=255|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3eerb4RTYF8C&pg=PA255|passage=In Maine, where as much as a quarter of the population has French ancestry, you may hear a stray hair called a ''couette'', and in parts of Ohio '''''please''''' is used in the same way as the German ''bitte'', to invite a person to repeat something just said — apparently a remnant of the bilingual schooling once available in Cincinnati.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Ellen McIntyre; Nancy Hulan; Vicky Layne|title=Reading Instruction for Diverse Classrooms: Research-Based, Culturally Responsive Practice|publisher=Guilford Press|isbn=1609180569|page=72|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=m7BAOCj8mHQC&pg=PA72|passage=Ellen grew up outside of Cincinnati and believed her own talk was the “norm,” while others were speakers of dialects. She was in graduate school before she learned that not all people say, '''''Please'''?'' to mean ''Can you repeat that?''}}

